course: dictionary
lesson: 5498
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word law can mean a rule that a government makes, or a rule that nature always seems to follow. Context tells you which meaning is right. «Drinking and driving is against the law and dangerous.» The clues are against and dangerous, so law means a rule everyone must follow.
explain: Law can also mean the whole system of rules, police and courts together. Ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then put your meaning in place of the word and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Law is sometimes a science word too. «The law of gravity» is not a rule people made. It describes something that always seems to be true, like things falling down when you drop them.
fact: Law means a rule made by a government that everyone in a country must follow.
fact: «Break the law» means to do something that the rules forbid.
fact: «By law» means required by a rule, as in «coach drivers are limited by law in how long they can drive without a break».
fact: In countries such as the UK, Canada and Australia, laws are made by Parliament; in the USA, they are made by Congress.
fact: A law of science, like the law of gravity, describes something that always seems to be true.
exercise: «Drinking and driving is against the law and dangerous.» What does law mean here? | A kind of loud noise | *A rule that everyone must follow | A type of food
exercise: «The law of gravity tells us that dropped things fall down.» What kind of law is this? | A law made by Parliament | *A law of science, a rule nature always seems to follow | A police car | A very old castle
exercise: Jess says, «Law is the subject I want to study to become a lawyer.» What does law mean here? | A law of science | A rule about speed limits | *The subject people study to become lawyers | A bus with seat belts
test: «Coach drivers are limited by law in how long they can drive without a break.» What does by law mean? | Without any help from anyone | Next to a river | *Required by a rule that must be followed | Very slowly
test: Which phrase means doing something the rules forbid? | Law and order | *Break the law | Obey the law | The law of gravity
exam: «The bill became law after Parliament passed it.» What does law mean here? | *A rule that a government has made and everyone must follow | A kind of bill that is sent in the post | A tree that grows tall in the forest | A science rule about falling objects
